#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119624 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6fe6c9-4c20-5481-a63f-ff9121824e94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Explicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "604fcbbb-6d34-5dca-8138-2dc99d524115" :question/math-academy-id "e-8875" :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=y^5-x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k} = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(y^5-x^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = u \\, \\mathbf{i} + v \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\big(v^5-u^2\\big) \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119625 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a1aa679a-bbe1-5682-bca4-5103f5717be0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8876" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\:  \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$x^2z-2y+z=6.$\n What is the third component of $\\mathbf{r}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}z-2y+z & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)z & =6+2y \\\\ z & =\\frac{2(y+3)}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\left\\langle x, \\: y, \\: \\dfrac{2(y+3)}{x^2+1} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) =\\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\:  {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{r}$ is ${\\color{black}\\dfrac{2(v+3)}{u^2+1}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "d298fa26-3b8f-5561-944c-ae6c919867b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8877" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\big\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: \\text{_____} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n${r^2}-4z^2=0$\nfor $z \\geq 0.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2}-4{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{r}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\geq 0.$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle r\\cos\\theta, \\: r\\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z= \\dfrac r2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =\\left\\langle r\\cos{\\theta}, \\: r\\sin{\\theta}, \\: {\\color{black}  \\dfrac r2} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}  \\dfrac r2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "d8feb600-a06a-581d-bd23-308c791d03e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8878" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: \\theta \\sin\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi, \\: \\theta \\sin\\phi \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi}.$\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle = \\big\\langle \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\rho\\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho=\\theta \\sin{\\phi},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ,\\,\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{black}\\theta \\cos\\theta \\sin^2 \\phi}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6fe6c9-4c20-5481-a63f-ff9121824e94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Explicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b9fd772-88bd-58a8-9b9f-0969f6743df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = vi + uj + (u^{3} - uv)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116495} {:answer/id #uuid "9094f663-7312-552f-83ba-167f3d3afaa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = uvi + uj + vk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116496} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1caadb-9820-5e00-a6d6-983f45ec4c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = uvi + vj + uk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116497} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2d2445-6d3e-5b74-b8a5-72d476038351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = ui + vj + (u^{3} - uv)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116498} {:answer/id #uuid "40f0b2dc-25e4-5e80-a854-3d43a95621d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = ui + (u^{3} - uv)j + vk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf2d2445-6d3e-5b74-b8a5-72d476038351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u, v) = ui + vj + (u^{3} - uv)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116498} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea8315bf-dd83-58c0-bbc4-548ff228e244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cced9a43-f637-5be6-a71f-17c0a9fd1fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103785" :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z={x}^{3}-xy.$" :question/worked-solution "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+({x}^{3}-xy)\\,k.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+({u}^{3}-uv)\\,k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2f354e-eec2-5530-a10e-30a37d3893da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{u^{3} + v^{2} - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116501} {:answer/id #uuid "afe87243-d931-580d-ade2-19768946db19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{3} + v^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116502} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8bb744-c85d-5a06-81ae-826b33095875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{3} + v^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116503} {:answer/id #uuid "4290540c-87f3-5bc5-8baa-3354806a6a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - u^{3} - v^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116504} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd4ffc4-4e50-5c3f-b606-741ea28a7ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{u^{3} + v^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e2f354e-eec2-5530-a10e-30a37d3893da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{u^{3} + v^{2} - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116501} :answer-field/id #uuid "902017c0-170f-5119-8d7b-141a6a2db943" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "414c5d5a-8607-5224-9289-1754189b03a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103787" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=2$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c9ce550-cd38-5439-b755-a164d5700ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116507} {:answer/id #uuid "a41cc995-31be-54e7-a867-e8a3d42e9ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ\\cos r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116508} {:answer/id #uuid "a6880998-e36f-5eee-b5b8-21d635e9bf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "θ\\sin r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116509} {:answer/id #uuid "13f85ddf-da5e-5e0c-b680-a15f0ef137bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\tan θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116510} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2ce324-c910-5777-86f5-8636cd07b499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c9ce550-cd38-5439-b755-a164d5700ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116507} :answer-field/id #uuid "591f0400-0bbb-5e3c-b610-29a09399e72c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abe0a7ed-085e-5846-929d-0cc0539c1f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-103789" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97f03a9e-1967-5781-a28c-59b82f5ce0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\cos^{2} θ\\sin ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116519} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ab1fb4-d72f-59fe-b69e-e0fe1d9373a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\sin θ\\cos ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116520} {:answer/id #uuid "13ade8c6-9c44-56b7-ba09-1e234048433a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\cos θ\\sin^{2} ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116521} {:answer/id #uuid "3d034e32-f7b4-5fd7-86da-8301bbadea92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\cos^{2} θ\\sin^{2} ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116522} {:answer/id #uuid "50c93f4c-0e84-56b0-b1d8-7abf19ece305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97f03a9e-1967-5781-a28c-59b82f5ce0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\cos^{2} θ\\sin ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116519} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c79168-aa3a-5cab-879c-432349a474c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c82e439b-a7c4-56f4-8daa-2bc41cb45c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-103795" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a04e5d0b-ce5e-5e54-869d-531ab14fdb5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\tan θ\\sin ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116525} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e2ff19-c061-539f-9d2b-2ccf884a6e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\cot θ\\sin ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116526} {:answer/id #uuid "d04ef4d7-8274-5e91-ae46-0929197c27b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\sec θ\\cos ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116527} {:answer/id #uuid "32e520cf-0c91-5f31-b1ad-2cb31f08f373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\csc θ\\sin ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116528} {:answer/id #uuid "6eefb807-9b5e-5c45-9259-222ecd626ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\csc θ\\cos ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eefb807-9b5e-5c45-9259-222ecd626ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\csc θ\\cos ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116529} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cf402b3-7c32-5942-821e-82c84e6c6af2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "26d2eff3-e64e-54a4-8d22-1bebcd2af070" :question/math-academy-id "q-103796" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{ _____ }⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6fe6c9-4c20-5481-a63f-ff9121824e94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Explicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c80d42f-0b98-5c7b-9fda-2a5c79a650cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{u+v}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314421} {:answer/id #uuid "a026580a-4d08-5b08-878e-5392a0c7d1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡uv⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314422} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d4aa1b-e246-5504-adf1-4122a400b544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{uv}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314423} {:answer/id #uuid "2293c2ed-ce74-5cd7-9843-57fa4ee53183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,uv⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314424} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfdab85-250e-55a8-b4a3-a46b82bc7199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨{e}^{u},\\,{e}^{v},\\,{e}^{uv}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8d4aa1b-e246-5504-adf1-4122a400b544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{uv}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314423} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec92c5d-ccbf-5459-a0b6-d47fc8c926b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5e0eded-4969-5923-ac06-88f8c9f75ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-104138" :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z={e}^{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,{e}^{xy}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{uv}⟩\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6fe6c9-4c20-5481-a63f-ff9121824e94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Explicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdec8f39-cb89-5b3e-8312-12ea2fa4e22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+v+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314428} {:answer/id #uuid "d6050f81-472f-530c-9015-3f2623dd099c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314429} {:answer/id #uuid "a852fca1-f79f-5dc3-9616-91b6b2702bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314430} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba8bfb6-0e48-54cf-91a5-cd501989bc27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314431} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1ce979-5244-5b08-93dd-e1755fcc360e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{e}^{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6050f81-472f-530c-9015-3f2623dd099c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/val