id "f1282973-dfce-58a5-8c96-2e494a0d97d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,(u+v)^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1282973-dfce-58a5-8c96-2e494a0d97d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,(u+v)^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314439} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e174ea6-d4d1-5e9a-b04d-d4cdba4b316f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "002dceea-15fa-5440-a7e7-d49fba0cc20a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104144" :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=(x+y)^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,(x+y)^{4}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,(u+v)^{4}⟩\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6fe6c9-4c20-5481-a63f-ff9121824e94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Explicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8212d4f2-6865-5b14-b1af-7962aec49356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314442} {:answer/id #uuid "74b9432e-0d40-5626-8321-75389dd513c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,\\sqrt[3]{u+v}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314443} {:answer/id #uuid "ef64fd63-10a0-51af-8ed6-14df92407724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨v,\\,u,\\,u-v⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314444} {:answer/id #uuid "050c8c39-1697-5cdb-b7a6-6ba9ad605971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{u+v}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314445} {:answer/id #uuid "504a1e97-9995-54bd-9a15-d9a53366e7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{u-v}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504a1e97-9995-54bd-9a15-d9a53366e7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{u-v}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314446} :answer-field/id #uuid "41986025-5bb4-5a4d-82cf-fc9d53447478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84e6bdff-147d-5fae-98b7-5329dc67f214" :question/math-academy-id "q-104147" :question/problem "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=\\sqrt[3]{x-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt[3]{x-y}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{u-v}⟩\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5fe475d-c261-590f-97f5-0d43bbda476d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314449} {:answer/id #uuid "812315f7-e33b-58f1-bd1f-9a263996b291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314450} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8a3e14-f471-5190-bced-8ee99352ad5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314451} {:answer/id #uuid "f19bd09b-ead0-5950-b159-2999152b5731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(u+v)^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314452} {:answer/id #uuid "4082770e-e194-5538-b403-21e32d71647d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{(u+v)^{3}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "812315f7-e33b-58f1-bd1f-9a263996b291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314450} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cdd6da0-a594-5eb9-b3cd-5276f69da0af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d37951d-f75b-50a7-a043-80a7f31b132e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104170" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2}=1$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5428c067-f0a9-5173-b055-49df0d695d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314456} {:answer/id #uuid "d56267fe-8124-581f-a027-3ef93fde911e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314457} {:answer/id #uuid "b6df7fa4-1196-5207-95e7-52b0cd7c693e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314458} {:answer/id #uuid "bac673aa-51f7-556c-8971-0df4408a1932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314459} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2a5195-90bc-5cd7-a852-77c0303955e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6df7fa4-1196-5207-95e7-52b0cd7c693e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314458} :answer-field/id #uuid "859c53b1-1fd6-50b7-937a-8d6cd49d6532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d57744a-d330-5f8c-832f-b2905e398bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104172" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{z} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a123db7e-45b9-5e2e-96af-60bded010298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}v-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314463} {:answer/id #uuid "dec0b316-ba93-5624-b046-41ef79d515d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{u-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314464} {:answer/id #uuid "b17b86ae-9653-528d-84f5-81dadceec85b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314465} {:answer/id #uuid "88ab374b-7c10-5bc6-b3db-64e594cddd5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{uv-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314466} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1be00c-efda-5c47-8544-37d401ce1b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e1be00c-efda-5c47-8544-37d401ce1b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314467} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce7a0ffd-6053-50c8-8065-a31a7c9b5ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d304d1bf-04b6-5fa3-8eec-a0e6476429ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-104181" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}y-{z}^{3}=3.$ \n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-{z}^{3} & =3 \\\\ {z}^{3} & ={x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}y-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}y-3}\\,k.\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272c698f-b7bf-5a67-87a0-7f316a861c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-3v+2u)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314470} {:answer/id #uuid "f8df0ad3-ef65-53ea-9361-b77bb8ff853f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3v+2u)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314471} {:answer/id #uuid "41d85f53-3254-55f8-91c7-f40bc557d750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314472} {:answer/id #uuid "13296ee7-6bce-54e9-a8d3-d4128344429f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3v-2u)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314473} {:answer/id #uuid "557309a4-9929-5f93-a122-3331f09ce821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-3v+2u)^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d85f53-3254-55f8-91c7-f40bc557d750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314472} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2ea5e2-8a2c-5178-a3f8-24e149094b01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e0e0f80-cd50-5f5b-a6b4-c3eb06298d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-104182" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2z} & =3y-2x \\\\ z & =\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5acaceba-650e-5c01-94ab-419680c223a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314477} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0daf52-e7c2-5a26-b898-cd31a91f186e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314478} {:answer/id #uuid "929b6f29-2ed5-514c-917a-48164435dab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314479} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff305e8-e8e5-578c-9279-9a3e545fea04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314480} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5d7d8a-6120-5098-b66c-b72b7a18bb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e0daf52-e7c2-5a26-b898-cd31a91f186e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314478} :answer-field/id #uuid "4620c5b4-d7f4-56dd-990b-363e6f10297a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5199fe2e-47c1-5f85-9855-f631b36b7e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-104270" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization \n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is  $r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659db62e-df43-5ca5-af17-4e0a2e45ca31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314484} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6588ac-c52b-5c95-b7ee-770b38121126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314485} {:answer/id #uuid "519b8e23-7e98-58fb-a872-2349d1055a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314486} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ada32b-7b7d-59c5-a767-4362f1b5f845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314487} {:answer/id #uuid "b073fa80-ae0a-5722-b190-a1777cc06ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b073fa80-ae0a-5722-b190-a1777cc06ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314488} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a045dd-59cc-593b-a258-8991a62630ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51f7a8a6-a740-5f12-9a11-a82ea66f3f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-104273" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}}={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta $\nfor $z\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={r}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta | \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$ Also, we drop the absolute value bars because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\ge 0$ for  $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$  \n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is ${r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35740613-2907-5ff2-90fa-8eb380d31223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314491} {:answer/id #uuid "7356dfe8-3a44-5c82-9f1a-c9327fd7779d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314492} {:answer/id #uuid "23bdfe5f-07a6-5760-a328-652c17e04271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314493} {:answer/id #uuid "11404ab1-e990-5fb4-87b3-9619ece6db4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314494} {:answer/id #uuid "4a77379b-39b7-5e79-ac43-b92e59d7e22f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7356dfe8-3a44-5c82-9f1a-c9327fd7779d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314492} :answer-field/id #uuid "874d6b06-8a01-5b16-8e6a-cfb022dfcf5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b29c6b5-a0c6-5fdd-b4f6-c711de17da5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104276" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15377cf2-f331-58da-83f6-db9c8fc1d53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314498} {:answer/id #uuid "96e349ba-cab9-5a08-962d-285810e237ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314499} {:answer/id #uuid "61160a07-f724-509c-8446-0138c7144884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314500} {:answer/id #uuid "86c9a62b-5bf3-5238-8be8-5b24315245c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314501} {:answer/id #uuid "af829500-ff59-593a-b825-57063834f264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15377cf2-f331-58da-83f6-db9c8fc1d53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314498} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe08c6c3-d8c1-5452-907a-8fc845e59ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dda73456-2544-5e38-8206-6e5fa605efcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-104278" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization \n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b00824a8-0540-5875-9e8e-8817a9eed237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314505} {:answer/id #uuid "091b82d5-eea2-5f5c-ad68-288ddf337de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314506} {:answer/id #uuid "8fdcdfe8-eb49-520d-895d-2f875a7d7f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314507} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a9f5bf-2019-5ccc-b514-818d617842fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314508} {:answer/id #uuid "c2865ff9-b565-5d24-9cfc-b9dade1e2bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "091b82d5-eea2-5f5c-ad68-288ddf337de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314506} :answer-field/id #uuid "08d0ed85-6489-5cb7-afbe-0c0669c8e9f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "96230e9a-9b2e-5ded-835d-50fdb79267c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104365" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{ _____ }⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "970d2b1a-4e14-56d6-8533-1eaeddbc716e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314512} {:answer/id #uuid "051d5993-7904-56ef-a271-e5d002615b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314513} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca84e6e-e108-5201-b564-b5082480b300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314514} {:answer/id #uuid "9ada14d3-b271-59e0-89b7-4f43c81854a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314515} {:answer/id #uuid "3f09c4a9-6b7c-511f-b8d2-a889e20d0541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ca84e6e-e108-5201-b564-b5082480b300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314514} :answer-field/id #uuid "f61fb015-9e76-50b9-92fa-0c9abe890de2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "025b4b30-6969-5e0b-82fb-0c3def5d3c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104372" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ea4a32f-e113-575c-b6b3-ff2c12045092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314519} {:answer/id #uuid "156e8b7c-6226-5b25-bd27-ba590e72f057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314520} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6d03ba-ab0f-5e38-b309-eef48666fb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314521} {:answer/id #uuid "79f0d568-ff39-5e22-b988-215a4af8e073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314522} {:answer/id #uuid "d40b9de3-3a8b-5cf6-8dc9-90c6c189b49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d40b9de3-3a8b-5cf6-8dc9-90c6c189b49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314523} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1bade2-c256-5dc0-b78d-5f94e7fb6f59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "027c00df-6ab8-583f-bb22-d5f3b64f0acb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104375" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $ϕ{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}]}
